<header class="mast">
  <div class="eyebrow"><span>ENG-2512</span><span>2026-11-20</span><span>Research only · no code changed</span></div>
  <h1>How MCP updates reach users, and what an API endpoint switch costs</h1>
  <p class="note">Input for the OpenSpec change <code>headless-mcp-retrieval</code> (Roam block <code>igzkQFr4r</code>), Decisions 1, 8 and 9.</p>
</header>

<section class="verdict">
  <h2>Keep the local npx <code>@latest</code> MCP from the spec. Ship a version header and an "upgrade required" error in its first release.</h2>
  <p>With <code>npx -y @discourse-graphs/mcp@latest</code>, a new npm release reaches every user the next time their agent client restarts the MCP process. Nobody has to run a command. The gap is everything else: users aren't told an update exists, some never restart, and we can't see who is behind. Each of the three things to add first takes about a day. The MCP sends its version on every API call, the API can refuse versions that are too old with a message the agent can relay, and the session state file keeps a backward-compatible format. Once they ship, endpoint changes become server-side decisions instead of hopes about user behavior.</p>
  <p>A remote (hosted HTTP) MCP is the only option where every user gets an update instantly. The spec rejected it on 2026-11-10 because of the hosted credential store it needs. Most of what it offers we already get: the MCP is a thin client, so retrieval fixes deploy with the website. The version header gets us most of the rest, which is knowing who is behind and being able to tell them, without hosting anything.</p>
  <p class="note">Small <u>source</u> toggles after claims hold where each one came from. You can read the brief straight through without opening them.</p>
</section>

<details class="sec" id="s1" open>
<summary><h2 data-n="1">How does an MCP update propagate to users, especially for a local MCP?</h2><span class="sub">What happens between <code>npm publish</code> and a user running the new code.</span></summary>
<div class="body">
<ul>
  <li><strong>A local MCP updates when its process restarts.</strong> The agent client starts the stdio process when it launches and keeps it running. A newly published version runs only after that process restarts.
    <details class="prov"><summary>source</summary>Claude Desktop: "completely quit Claude Desktop and restart it", modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/connect-local-servers. Claude Code: /mcp → Reconnect, code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp. Codex: no reconnect command is documented; the app has Restart. Via research subagent.</details></li>
  <li><strong><code>@latest</code> re-checks npm on every launch.</strong> npx looks up the tag's manifest with <code>preferOnline: true</code>. If the resolved version differs from its cached copy, it installs the new one. A restart is all the user has to do.
    <details class="prov"><summary>source</summary>Verified myself on npm 10.9.8: <code>libnpmexec/lib/index.js:27</code> (<code>pacote.manifest(spec, {...flatOptions, preferOnline: true})</code>) and <code>:40</code> (tag specs skip the "anything in the tree matches" shortcut). The subagent reports the same logic in v9.9.3 through current.</details></li>
  <li><strong>A bare package name is weaker than <code>@latest</code>.</strong> Without a tag, npx runs a copy installed in the project or globally, even an old one. Exact pins never update. Setup docs should always say <code>@latest</code> and never <code>npm i -g</code>.
    <details class="prov"><summary>source</summary>npx docs: "Package names provided without a specifier will be matched with whatever version exists in the local project", docs.npmjs.com/cli/v11/commands/npx. The stale-cache bug for ranges was fixed in Feb 2025 (npm/cli#7838).</details></li>
  <li><strong>Nobody is told.</strong> No client shows "update available" for a local MCP. No popular server we checked warns users who are out of date. If we want to know or tell, the server has to do it itself.
    <details class="prov"><summary>source</summary>Subagent survey of Playwright, Sentry and GitHub MCP servers. Absence of evidence, not proof of absence.</details></li>
  <li><strong>We can't hold users back either.</strong> <code>@latest</code> delivers a breaking release on the next restart. Users have no grace period, so the state file in <code>~/.discourse-graphs/mcp/</code> must stay readable by every future version.</li>
  <li><strong>Old versions linger.</strong> These users run old code indefinitely:
    <ul>
      <li>users who keep their client open for weeks;</li>
      <li>users who pinned a version;</li>
      <li>users whose npx resolves an old global install.</li>
    </ul>
    So "every user updates" never happens. The API has to tolerate older versions until we can measure that they're gone.</li>
</ul>
<p>The MCP spec's <code>notifications/tools/list_changed</code> doesn't help here. It refreshes the tool list inside a running process. It never swaps in new code, and a new npx version brings its tool definitions with it at startup.
<details class="prov"><summary>source</summary>MCP spec, tools and caching pages (modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/latest/server/tools). Client support for list_changed is uneven: Claude Code documents it, and Cursor forum reports say it's ignored. Via subagent.</details></p>
</div>
</details>

<details class="sec" id="s2">
<summary><h2 data-n="2">Distribution options compared</h2><span class="sub">Six ways to ship the MCP to public users, by how updates arrive and who has to act.</span></summary>
<div class="body">
<div class="tbl"><table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>npx <code>@latest</code> (spec today)</th><th>npx exact pin</th><th>.mcpb bundle</th><th>Claude Code plugin</th><th>git checkout</th><th>Remote HTTP</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Update arrives</td><td><span class="pill g">auto</span>next client restart</td><td><span class="pill b">never</span>user edits config</td><td><span class="pill b">manual</span>download and reinstall</td><td><span class="pill w">opt-in</span>auto-update is off for third-party marketplaces</td><td><span class="pill b">manual</span>git pull, install, restart</td><td><span class="pill g">instant</span>on deploy</td></tr>
<tr><td>User action</td><td>Restart client</td><td>Edit config, restart</td><td>Download and double-click</td><td>Toggle auto-update once, or run <code>/plugin update</code></td><td>Terminal commands</td><td>None, sometimes a reconnect</td></tr>
<tr><td>First install</td><td><span class="pill w">medium</span>paste a config block and run connect</td><td><span class="pill w">medium</span>same</td><td><span class="pill g">easy</span>bundled Node, settings form</td><td><span class="pill g">easy</span>Claude Code only</td><td><span class="pill b">hard</span>developers only</td><td><span class="pill g">easy</span>URL plus OAuth</td></tr>
<tr><td>Can hold users back</td><td><span class="pill b">no</span></td><td><span class="pill g">yes</span></td><td><span class="pill g">yes</span></td><td><span class="pill g">yes</span></td><td><span class="pill g">yes</span></td><td><span class="pill b">no</span>one version for all</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fits our design</td><td><span class="pill g">yes</span>matches Decision 9</td><td><span class="pill w">partly</span>stale users pile up</td><td><span class="pill w">partly</span>Claude Desktop only, directory closed to new submissions</td><td><span class="pill w">partly</span>can wrap npx <code>@latest</code></td><td><span class="pill b">no</span>for public users</td><td><span class="pill b">no for v0</span>needs a hosted credential store</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<ul>
  <li><strong>.mcpb is an install aid, not an update channel.</strong> Directory listings once auto-updated, but the Claude directory no longer accepts MCPB submissions. A self-distributed <code>.mcpb</code> needs a manual reinstall for every update.
    <details class="prov"><summary>source</summary>claude.com/docs/connectors/building/mcpb.md: "Desktop extension listings in the directory are deprecated, and the directory no longer accepts MCPB submissions." support.claude.com/en/articles/10949351. Via subagent; not checked by me.</details></li>
  <li><strong>A plugin can wrap npx.</strong> A Claude Code plugin whose MCP config runs <code>npx …@latest</code> gets easy installs while code updates still arrive through npm. This is worth considering after v0.</li>
  <li><strong>The registry helps people find the server, not update it.</strong> The official MCP Registry is metadata pointing at npm, and no client auto-updates from it. Smithery shut down its hosted services on 2026-10-31.
    <details class="prov"><summary>source</summary>modelcontextprotocol.io/registry/about ("not intended to be directly consumed by host applications"); smithery.ai/future. Via subagent.</details></li>
  <li><strong>Others are moving to remote.</strong> Sentry labels its npx server "Legacy" in favor of a hosted server. GitHub recommends its remote server first and keeps renamed tools as aliases. Playwright stays on npx <code>@latest</code>.
    <details class="prov"><summary>source</summary>github.com/getsentry/sentry-mcp, github.com/github/github-mcp-server, github.com/microsoft/playwright-mcp. Via subagent.</details></li>
</ul>
</div>
</details>

<details class="sec" id="s3">
<summary><h2 data-n="3">How does updating an MCP tool work? How much work is it to move to a new API endpoint?</h2><span class="sub">The case of <code>get_node</code> moving from <code>api/v1/internal/nodes/[nodeId]</code> to <code>api/v1/nodes/[nodeId]</code>, which returns a different shape.</span></summary>
<div class="body">
<div class="callout"><strong>The internal route stops being internal once a published npm build calls it.</strong> Installed copies keep calling it, and we can't update or list them. From then on it falls under the same additive-only rule as <code>/api/v1</code>. Either don't ship a public build against an <code>/internal</code> route, or treat it as a versioned public contract from day one.</div>
<p>The spec today already points the MCP at <code>/api/v1/nodes/{rid}</code> (Decision 8). So this scenario is a future migration, not the v0 plan.</p>
<h3>Why the tool's output shape is a hard contract</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong><code>outputSchema</code> is enforced.</strong> The TypeScript SDK server checks <code>structuredContent</code> against the declared schema before sending, and the v1.x SDK client throws on a mismatch. An undeclared shape change becomes a protocol error.
    <details class="prov"><summary>source</summary>MCP spec, tools page: server MUST conform, client SHOULD validate. TS SDK v1.x <code>src/client/index.ts</code> throws McpError "Structured content does not match the tool's output schema". The subagent read the source; v2 client behavior was not verified.</details></li>
  <li><strong>Agents depend on field names.</strong> Skills, prompts and chained calls refer to fields such as the node title and <code>rid</code>. If a field is renamed under the same tool name, nothing reports an error. The agent just gets worse results.</li>
  <li><strong>The text part matters too.</strong> Many clients pass only the short text form to the model, so changing that text changes agent behavior even when <code>structuredContent</code> stays the same.</li>
  <li><strong>The spec has no per-tool versioning.</strong> Versioning shows up in the tool name (<code>get_node_v2</code>) or in an opt-in parameter.
    <details class="prov"><summary>source</summary>Subagent reading of the MCP spec 2026-07-28 revision. It also reports that this revision replaced <code>initialize</code> with per-result <code>serverInfo</code> in <code>_meta</code>. I haven't checked that myself, and the recommendation doesn't depend on it.</details></li>
</ul>
<h3>Three ways to do the switch</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>A. Change behind the route</th><th>B. New MCP release with an adapter</th><th>C. New MCP release with the new shape</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>What changes</td><td>The internal route calls the same code as the public one and maps the result to the old shape</td><td>The tool calls the new route and maps the new shape to the old tool output</td><td>The tool exposes the new shape, with new <code>outputSchema</code>, description and text form</td></tr>
<tr><td>Touched</td><td>Route handler and a contract test against the old Zod schema</td><td>Tool handler, adapter, fixtures from both routes, integration test, minor npm release</td><td>Tool handler, schema, description, docs, skills that use old fields, evals, major npm release</td></tr>
<tr><td>Old installed copies</td><td><span class="pill g">unaffected</span></td><td><span class="pill g">unaffected</span>still call the old route</td><td><span class="pill w">unaffected</span>still call the old route; upgraded users' skills break silently</td></tr>
<tr><td>Old route lives</td><td>Indefinitely, as a stable façade</td><td>Until the version header shows no callers</td><td>Until the version header shows no callers</td></tr>
<tr><td>MCP release needed</td><td><span class="pill g">no</span></td><td><span class="pill w">yes</span>minor</td><td><span class="pill b">yes</span>major</td></tr>
<tr><td>Effort</td><td><span class="pill g">S</span>a day or two</td><td><span class="pill w">M</span>about a week; L if the public route lacks fields the old shape had</td><td><span class="pill b">L</span>most of the cost is docs and communication</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<details class="prov"><summary>source</summary>Judgement. The S/M/L estimates come from the research subagent and me, inferred from the touched-file list, not measured. B grows to L when the old shape's fields must be derived or set to null.</details>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Default to A.</strong> It works whenever the new route's data is a superset of the old route's. It also matches Decision 1, where fixes ship by deploying the website.</li>
  <li><strong>Use B to retire a route.</strong> It only works once the version header tells us when old callers are gone. Without the header, the old route lives forever, or we accept that it breaks for an unknown number of users.</li>
  <li><strong>Use C only under a new tool name,</strong> or behind an opt-in <code>format</code> parameter. Never swap the shape under an unchanged name and description.</li>
  <li><strong>Send deprecation signals the MCP can read.</strong> Put <code>Deprecation</code> (RFC 9745) and <code>Sunset</code> (RFC 8594) headers on old routes. After the sunset, return a JSON error body. Only our MCP sees these headers, so it has to log them or turn them into a notice.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</details>

<details class="sec" id="s4">
<summary><h2 data-n="4">Proposed edits to the OpenSpec change</h2><span class="sub">Small additions to <code>headless-mcp-retrieval</code> that make the above possible from v0.</span></summary>
<div class="body">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Decision 8: add a version header.</strong> The MCP sends <code>User-Agent: discourse-graphs-mcp/&lt;version&gt;</code> on every call, and the API logs the version next to the request ID and operation. That log is how we decide when a route can be retired.</li>
  <li><strong>Decision 8: add an <code>upgrade_required</code> error code.</strong> Give it a safe message telling the user to restart their agent client. The MCP passes it through as a tool error, like other codes, so the agent can relay it. Use HTTP 410 or 422, not 426, because 426 requires an <code>Upgrade</code> header.</li>
  <li><strong>Decision 9: version the state file.</strong> Add a <code>version</code> field to <code>~/.discourse-graphs/mcp/&lt;name&gt;.json</code>, and require each release to read every earlier format.</li>
  <li><strong>Task 5.4: add verification.</strong> Unit tests that the header is sent and that <code>upgrade_required</code> maps to a tool error.</li>
  <li><strong>Task 6.2: add a setup README line.</strong> Tell users to restart the client to update, and never to install the package globally.</li>
</ul>
<p>None of these changes the four routes or tools. They are additive, and they're cheapest to add before the first public install exists.</p>
</div>
</details>

<details class="sec" id="s5">
<summary><h2 data-n="5">Open items</h2><span class="sub">What couldn't be settled, and what a wrong guess costs.</span></summary>
<div class="body">
<ul>
  <li><strong>How long npm keeps serving old package metadata after a publish.</strong> Not tested. If it's slow, a fast-follow fix takes longer to reach users. The cost is low, because the API can patch around it.</li>
  <li><strong>What npx <code>@latest</code> does offline.</strong> Not tested. If it fails rather than falling back to the cached copy, the MCP won't start on a plane. The cost is low, because retrieval needs the network anyway.</li>
  <li><strong>Codex and Claude Desktop tool caching.</strong> We don't know whether they honor <code>list_changed</code> or for how long they cache tool lists. Codex can start from a cached tool list. If that list is stale, the agent may briefly see old tool definitions after an update. The cost is medium for option C and none for A or B.</li>
  <li><strong>Whether Anthropic's official plugin directory auto-updates by default.</strong> Unverified. It matters only if we choose the plugin route after v0.</li>
  <li><strong>Startup cost.</strong> <code>@latest</code> does a registry round trip on every launch, and Codex's default startup timeout is 10 seconds. Not measured. Check it during task 7.1.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</details>

